/* The header for this test is at the BOTTOM of this file, below the script.
   It is there because a constant inside a leading comment can swallow the
   rest of the file in the engine - see RMSTEST_56 and the directory README.
   Nothing below the script can do any harm. */

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement
  ai_info_map_type ARABIA 0 0

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain SNOW

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>


<OBJECTS_GENERATION>


<CONNECTION_GENERATION>

/*
RMSTEST_54a - does the map-type declaration take three arguments or four?
Feeds: reference/data/language.json, parser diagnostic RMS0201,
       docs/known-issues.md BUG-003's one remaining undetermined row

QUESTION
The declaration that tells the computer players what kind of map this is takes a
name and two flags, and the guide documents a THIRD trailing flag with a default
of zero. Ten corpus maps, eight of them official, write only three tokens.

The parser warns on all ten. The warning is deliberately kept, because this
project's rule is that the guide licenses an omissible argument with a SENTENCE,
never with a default notation, and there is no such sentence here.

It cannot be settled from shipped maps, and that is not a matter of sample size.
The trailing flag is documented as non-functional in this edition, so the two
forms are indistinguishable in play and NO shipped script could ever have
revealed which is correct. Fifty-two independent uses were once read as evidence
and reverted the same day for exactly this reason.

So the only thing that can answer it is whether the ENGINE EATS THE NEXT TOKEN.

METHOD
Put the three-token form at the very end of the player setup section, with the
land generation header immediately after it, and nothing else between.

  arity three -> the header survives, the land section opens, the base terrain
                 line runs, and the map comes out SNOW.
  arity four  -> the header is swallowed as the missing flag. The land section
                 never opens, the base terrain line is orphaned, and the map
                 comes out as a blank default.

A blank default map is the same signature as any other fatal script error, which
is why 54b exists: it is this file with the declaration line deleted and nothing
else changed. Run both.

  54a snow, 54b snow  -> arity three. Mark the argument optional in
                         language.json, stop warning on ten maps, close the last
                         undetermined row in BUG-003.
  54a blank, 54b snow -> arity four. The warning is a true positive on all ten
                         maps, including eight official ones, and it stays.
  both blank          -> something unrelated is wrong with the pair. Fix the map
                         before reading either.

Using a section header as the swallowed token is deliberate. Any ordinary
statement would be swallowed too, but the consequence would be one missing
command and a map that still looks plausible. Swallowing a section header takes
the whole rest of the script with it, which is an outcome nobody can misread as
a near miss.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), any player count. ONE RUN EACH.

READ IT WITH
  Look at the map. Snow or not snow is the entire reading and it does not need
  the probe. Export anyway if you want the count on record.
*/
